  <body>
    <h1 class="allcaps center">Paul M. Rodriguez</h1>
    <div class="huge center allcaps" style="margin-bottom: 0.618em">
      Works
    </div>

    <section>
        <p>Paul M. Rodriguez is an <a href="#ru">essayist</a>, <a href="#ec">novelist</a>, <a href="#rambler">scholar</a>, and <a href="#oss">programmer</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section id=ru>
        <h1><i><a href=<#rubk>>The Ruricolist</a></i> · Essay series</h1>

        <img src="img/ruricolist-cover.png"
             style="float: left; padding: 0 2em 2em 0">

        <p><i>The Ruricolist</i> is a single-author essay series, broadly philosophical in content. Originally <a href="https://the.ruricolist.com">online</a>, it is now also available in <a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/1794566694">print</a>.</p>

        <p><i>The Ruricolist</i> is mostly essays – <a href="http://the.ruricolist.com/search/label/essays">157 of them</a>, such as:</p>

        <ul>
            <#item "http://the.ruricolist.com/2009/01/uncut-pages.html"|Uncut pages|– on writing>
            <#item "http://the.ruricolist.com/2011/08/cell-intelligence_17.html"|Cell intelligence|– on ideas>
            <li>
                <a href="http://the.ruricolist.com/2011/05/laika.html">
                    Laika</a>
                <i>– on dogs</i>
            </li>
            <li>
                <a href="http://the.ruricolist.com/2009/11/xerxes.html">
                    Xerxes</a>
                <i>– on history</i>
            </li>
            <li>
                <a href="http://the.ruricolist.com/2009/03/civilizations.html">
                    Civilizations</a>
                <i>– on history</i>
            </li>
            <li>
                <a href="http://the.ruricolist.com/2009/10/technology.html">
                    Lead</a>
                <i>– on human nature</i>
            </li>
            <li>
                <a href="http://the.ruricolist.com/2010/04/roadkill.html">
                    Roadkill</a>
                <i>– on death</i>
            </li>
        </ul>

        <p>The rest of the 234 numbers is made up of <a href="http://the.ruricolist.com/search/label/tales">stories</a>,
            <a href="http://the.ruricolist.com/search/label/fables">fables</a>, <a href="http://the.ruricolist.com/search/label/satires">satires</a>, <a href="http://the.ruricolist.com/search/label/monologues">monologues</a>, <a href="http://the.ruricolist.com/search/label/nondefinitions">nondefinitions,</a> and others.</p>

        <p><i>The Ruricolist</i> can be read at <a href="http://the.ruricolist.com">the.ruricolist.com</a>, or as a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/1794566694">book</a>.</p>

    </section>

    <section id=ec>
        <h1><i><a href="http://www.amazon.com/The-Endless-City-Paul-Rodriguez/dp/130081098X/">The Endless City</a></i> · Novel</h1>

        <p><i>The Endless City</i> is a novel. The setting is what might be called post-apocalyptic, but not in the wake of destruction. What kind of society would people have, in the ruins of a world they could no longer remember or understand?
        </p>

        <div style="display: flex">
            <div style="margin: 0 auto;">
                <iframe width="560" height="315" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/SWpFwGcjzZ8?si=kReQAJc2zojl-L9E&rel=0" title="YouTube video player" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" allowfullscreen></iframe></div>
        </div>
        <p>Readers frequently compare <i>The Endless City</i> to Cormac McCarthy and Jorge Luis Borges.</p>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <a href="http://www.amazon.com/Endless-City-Paul-M-Rodriguez-ebook/dp/B00AQR4TRC/">
                    <i>The Endless City</i> (Kindle)
                </a>
            </li>
            <li>
                <a href="https://amazon.com/Endless-City-Paul-M-Rodriguez/dp/1650625111/">
                    <i>The Endless City</i> (Print)
                </a>
            </li>
        </p>
        </ul>

    </section>

    <section id=rambler>
        <h1><a href="http://www.amazon.com/Rambler-Samuel-Johnson-ebook/dp/B006U9DDXG/"><i>The Rambler</i></a> · Editor</h1>

        <img src="img/rambler-cover.jpg"
             style="float: left; padding: 0 2em 2em 0">

      <p>The only complete electronic edition of Samuel Johnson's <i>Rambler</i>
      available anywhere online, with all classical quotations (Latin
      and Greek) checked and corrected against their original sources.</p>

      <ul>
        <#item "http://www.amazon.com/Rambler-Samuel-Johnson-ebook/dp/B006U9DDXG/"|The Rambler|(Kindle)>
        <#item "http://www.barnesandnoble.com/w/rambler-samuel-johnson/1100108921?ean=2940150724181"|The Rambler|(Nook)>
      </ul>

      <p>This edition is now also available in print (as a single, complete volume):</p>

      <ul>
      <#item "http://www.amazon.com/Rambler-Samuel-Johnson/dp/1507724799/"|The Rambler|(Print)>
      </ul>

</section>

<section id=oss>
    <h1><a href="https://github.com/ruricolist">Open Source Development</a></h1>

    <p>I have written many open-source libraries, principally in Common Lisp.</p>

    <p>The most popular are <a href="https://github.com/ruricolist/spinneret">Spinneret</a>,
        an HTML generator; <a href="https://github.com/ruricolist/serapeum">Serapeum</a>,
        a utility library; <a href="https://github.com/ruricolist/overlord">Overlord</a>,
        a build system; and <a href="https://github.com/ruricolist/vernacular">Vernacular</a>,
        a module system for embedded languages.</p>

    <p>More open-source work can be found at:</p>

    <ul>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/ruricolist">GitHub </a></li>
        <li><a href="https://gitlab.com/ruricolist">GitLab </a></li>
    </ul>

    <p>I also wrote articles blog about programming:</p>

    <ul>
        <li> <a href="https://etc.ruricolist.com">&c. – Notes and digressions</a></li>
    </ul>

</section>

